  "case": "nested_class_calls",
  "why": "N17 (SSPN-73): a bare constructor call to a nested class. Kotlin scopes a nested class over its whole outer class, so `Line()` written in `Order`, in a class nested in `Order`, or two levels down is `Order.Line`; the call pass built `<package>.Line` from the simple name, an id that exists nowhere, and an external placeholder of that name appeared. Controls in the same file: `Order()` written inside `Order.Line` is the top-level `Order`; a nested `Cart.Box` shadows the top-level `Box` only inside `Cart` (`Shelf` still gets `Box`); `Order.Line()` from a top-level function was already right.",
